    "article": "**Commission Plan Design: Why Simplicity Often Beats Sophistication**\n\nMany sales organizations fall into the same trap. They build commission plans that are technically elegant but practically confusing. The result? Sales reps spend more time calculating their potential earnings than actually selling.\n\nGood commission plan design starts with one question: what behavior do you want to drive? If the answer is \"close more deals,\" then the plan should reward closed deals — clearly, directly, and without too many conditions. Every time you add a modifier, an accelerator tier, or a product-specific multiplier, you create cognitive load for the sales team. Research from the Alexander Group, a sales consulting firm, suggests that reps who cannot explain their own compensation plan in two minutes are significantly less motivated by it.\n\nConsider a mid-sized SaaS company with three revenue segments: new business, renewals, and upsell. A common mistake is to assign different commission rates to each segment without explaining the strategic logic. If renewals pay 3% and new business pays 8%, reps will naturally prioritize new business — even when the company's actual retention problem is more urgent. Commission rates send signals. Sales leaders need to design those signals intentionally.\n\nQuota attainment thresholds are another critical design decision. Most plans pay nothing below 50% of quota, then apply a standard rate from 50% to 100%, and then accelerate above 100%. This \"three-zone\" structure is widely used because it protects company margin while still motivating reps across different performance levels. However, the threshold percentages must reflect realistic expectations. Setting the floor too high discourages underperformers before they can recover. Setting it too low removes urgency.\n\nTiming also matters. Plans paid monthly create more frequent feedback loops than plans paid quarterly. For early-stage companies or new sales hires, monthly payouts can reinforce positive selling habits faster. For enterprise sales teams with longer deal cycles, quarterly payouts may better match the rhythm of the business.\n\nFinally, commission plans must be documented in plain language. Legal precision is important, but clarity is essential. If a rep needs to ask their manager how to interpret a clause every time they close a deal, the plan is not working. Some companies now publish short video explainers alongside written plan documents. This combination reduces confusion and builds trust with the sales team.\n\nThe best commission plans are not the most complex ones. They are the ones that make the right behavior obvious, predictable, and rewarding.",
